22:47:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right to push back, and your examples are perfect: the dodo and passenger pigeon are indeed species where practical certainty is so high that “no doubt about it” is a perfectly reasonable statement. My previous answer may have sounded too agnostic by treating all extinction declarations as equally tentative, when in reality there’s a vast spectrum of confidence. The key is that **the same scientific method is used, but the evidence threshold is overwhelmingly met** for species like these, whereas for tiny insects it almost never is. Here’s why the dodo and passenger pigeon stand in stark contrast to most insects, and how that translates into genuine verification. ### Why we can be practically certain about the dodo and passenger pigeon These species share a set of traits that make their absence definitive in a way that’s impossible for a 2mm soil mite: 1. **Extreme conspicuousness** - **Dodo:** A large, flightless, ground-dwelling bird that was unafraid of humans. It couldn’t hide. Sailors literally walked up to it. - **Passenger pigeon:** Once the most numerous bird in North America, with flocks that darkened the sky for hours. They nested in massive colonies spanning hundreds of square miles. Their sheer abundance and visibility meant any surviving population would be impossible to miss. 2. **Thoroughly inhabited, accessible habitat** - Mauritius (the dodo’s only home) is a small island that has been intensively explored and inhabited for centuries. There is no remote, inaccessible interior where a breeding population could have escaped notice. - The passenger pigeon’s range was across the heavily populated eastern and central United States and Canada. It was a communal bird that relied on large groups and mast crops in forests that are now crisscrossed by roads, farms, and cities. A single living bird would cause a national sensation. 3. **Unambiguous historical records and relentless searching** - The dodo was gone by the late 17th century, with the last widely accepted sighting around 1662. For over 350 years, not one credible observation has been made despite intense interest, museum expeditions, and amateur naturalists combing the island. - The last known passenger pigeon, Martha, died in the Cincinnati Zoo in 1914. Ornithologists had been desperately searching for wild flocks for decades before that, and the offer of substantial rewards for confirmed sightings turned up nothing but misidentifications. 4. **No credible alternative explanations for absence** - Both species had specific, well-documented ecological needs. The dodo depended on a particular forest floor niche on a single island. The passenger pigeon required enormous, synchronized communal breeding events to perpetuate its population. Even if a handful survived in secret, they would be below the viable population size for such a social breeder. The species was functionally extinct long before the last individual died. When the IUCN evaluates such a case, the “exhaustive surveys” criterion is met not just by a few field seasons of entomologists with traps, but by **centuries of continuous observation by millions of people** in the exact locations where the species once lived. The absence of a single confirmed record in that time, for an animal that was impossible to overlook, makes the probability of persistence effectively zero. ### So, what does “verified” really mean? It means the same thing for the dodo as for a rare insect: **the accumulation of negative evidence reaches a point where any remaining doubt is unreasonable.** For the dodo, that point was reached long ago because the signal of its existence would have been deafening. For a minuscule insect that only ever lived on a single remote mountain, our ability to generate enough negative evidence is drastically limited—hence the residual doubt. To be precise, even for the dodo, a philosopher could argue we can’t have 100% deductive certainty. But science doesn’t deal in that kind of absolute proof; it deals in evidence beyond reasonable doubt. The dodo and passenger pigeon are extinct with the same level of confidence that the sun will rise tomorrow. That’s verification in any meaningful sense. ### Why this translates poorly to insects Your original question about small creatures like insects is exactly where the contrast becomes sharp. An insect can lack every one of those traits that made the dodo’s extinction undeniable: - It might be **cryptic**, nocturnal, and mistaken for other species. - Its **habitat** might be a single patch of leaf litter on an unsurveyed slope. - The **observers** (specialists) are rare, and the search window might be a few hours per year. - There is no centuries-long baseline of millions of laypeople looking. So when an entomologist declares “no reasonable doubt,” they are applying the exact same logic but with a fraction of the data. The doubt remains more reasonable. That’s why insect extinctions are far more frequently overturned by rediscoveries, and why many are listed as “Possibly Extinct” rather than fully extinct. **In short: we can and do verify extinctions with near-absolute practical certainty when the species is as big, loud, and detectable as a dodo or passenger pigeon in a well-watched world. The very same methodology simply can’t deliver that level of certainty for most insects, no matter how rigorously we apply it.**
